+
+## Manually maintained configuration list
+
+The abbreviated harness reads [`_tests-RobustModem.md`](../_tests-RobustModem.md)
+on every invocation, preserving order and duplicate entries and recording its
+SHA-256. A malformed, incomplete, or mid-run changed list fails the run. The
+output includes one `unsupported` record for each current entry; these records
+are **not simulations of those configurations**. The existing four-kernel
+fixture has no validated adapter for the list, and the acquisition and tracking
+stages are not an integrated receiver. Null values are unresolved, not zero or
+a pass. Changes to the list need a validated adapter that honors every selected
+impairment and rejects unsupported values rather than silently substituting a
+fixture.
+
+A future case-level implementation must predeclare its small simulation strata,
+seeds, confidence/stop rule and workspace budget. Measure receiver process CPU
+seconds after preparation per second of received baseband, including all
+receiver threads; separately record wall time and actual thread limits. A value
+of 1 CPU-second/second uses one fully occupied CPU thread, and 0.1 uses a tenth
+of one; available multi-thread ceiling and scheduling/headroom depend on the
+host. This differs from wall-time real-time margin when more than one thread is
+used. Report DSP workspace estimate and budget separately from process RSS,
+preparation cost, actual Helper and total three-carrier DATA C/N0 (dB/1 Hz), an
+uncertainty-supported target for greater than 90% message success, and the
+single-bit and literal `fox` transmission calendars. Account for noise density,
+framing, encryption, impairments and failure cases; do not infer these from
+power, isolated kernels, or an unqualified model alone. Report preparation as
+unresolved if the actual path is unavailable. Any extrapolation requires the
+same stated geometry and applicable holdout; an isolated-kernel result does not
+supply end-to-end CPU or physical-success estimates.
